{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 7. Apply a downward level shift (increasing with horizon) to the entire path.",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miscalibration: during sustained sell-offs the model assumes too much rebound/mean reversion; realized paths keep drifting lower. Reduce mean-reversion strength and allow continued downside momentum when the last 2–4 weeks trend is negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error increases with lead time (small near days 1–3, large by days 10–30). Use a horizon-scaled correction (e.g., minimal adjustment short-term, progressively larger downward adjustment long-term).",
    "Overshoot after local upticks: after short-term bounces in history, the model projects higher follow-through than realized. Damp the response to recent positive returns (cap upside slope) when medium-term trend remains down.",
    "Volatility/level mismatch: the model keeps prices in a higher band (often low-70s) while realized trades in mid-60s. Re-anchor forecasts toward the recent realized distribution (e.g., pull the forecast mean toward the last 10–15 day average/median)."
  ]
}
